    "url": "/teaching/ai-solutions/example.html#section-1",
    "body": "Make the example usable by another person: provide this source card, an intake sheet, a draft-and-source table, the review checklist, an exception note, and a delivery record. Number the version and label who performs each step. A slide showing those items without providing them is not a working kit.\n\nKeep four datasets separate\n\nWhat each supplied dataset can supportDataset | Values | Evidence limit |\n\nDiscovery | Eight waits; six report repeated delay; three would try a pilot. | Supports questions about this fictional sample, not demand in a population. |\n\nProcessing benchmark | Same 12 cases: manual 96 minutes and 12 acceptable replies; unchecked AI 24 and 9; reviewed AI 84 and 12. | Supplied performance comparison, not your actual user test. |\n\nMonthly planning scenario | 1,200 eligible replies; 50% planned adoption; one minute released per assisted reply; $24/hour capacity value; $180 tools plus $60 support monthly. | A conditional forecast. Adoption, transfer of timing, and costs require verification. |\n\nAdoption cohort | 12 invited, 9 started, 6 completed, 4 returned within 14 days after invitation. | Return means completing another task in that same 14-day window; not a forecast for all coordinators. |\n\nYour actual evidence starts with what you do. Use the packet to practice and build. Then ask a classmate, instructor, or approved partner user to perform a bounded task with your version. Record the real attempt, assistance, result, revision, and retest. Leave fields blank until the event happens. A self-review is useful preparation but is not another person’s user test.\n\nStart the course →"
